      "text": "We factor the left side to obtain \\[\\sin(x+y)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y)=(\\sin\\frac x2\\cos\\frac x2+\\cos\\frac x2\\sin\\frac y2)+(\\sin\\frac y2\\cos\\frac y2+\\cos\\frac y2\\sin\\frac x2),\\]before writing \\[\\cos\\left(\\frac x2+\\frac y2-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\leq\\sin\\frac x2+\\cos\\frac y2.\\]This is irreducibly easy, and clearly becomes true when $x=y$, so the solutions are simply $y=\\pi,0+\\pi k,$ where $k$ is a positive integer. $\\diamondsuit$ An easier solution which takes much less explanation uses linear algebra. \n\nLet the matrix (wlog, square) $A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin x & \\sin y \\end{bmatrix}$, and the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\r\n\\cos(xay)\r\n\\end{bmatrix}$. We claim that for any $x,y\\in \\Bbb R$, $\\sin(x+y)\\le\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$ if and only if $A<v$. Here's a sketch of why I claim it: as $A-\\sin(x)v=v-\\sin(x)y$ is the \"difference\" between a given function and a linear function (this is the way you evaluate an ordinary differential equation vs get a fundamental solution), the condition $\\sin(x+y)\\le\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$ tells you when that linear function is increasing. But that $A<v$ is equivalent to that difference being positive everywhere. Now the formula $A<v$ amounts to boxed-minimizing $\\Bbb E(vA)$. As $v$ has \"nonnegative entries\", this is maximized when $v$ is equal to the column space of $A$, which is a permutation matrix (for some underlying permutation of length 2). Hence, your criterion is fulfilled if $y$ is equal to a constant multiple of $\\pi$, as Snuke said. Your problem is superficially about inequalities; in reality you only get a solution if $y$ is an integer multiple of $2\\pi r \\ \\ \\ (r\\in\\Bbb Z$, since if $2a$ is not trivial, it is not possible for $2a+x$ to equal $x$ entirely in the sine function terms on the LHS of $sin(x+y)<=sin(x)+sin(y)$. (this can be proven with induction). - Prove by induction. - Show that for any arbitrary positive y, however much non-trivial it is, the inequality is true.",
