      "text": "In order for the inequality to hold for all $x$, the left hand side should be less than or equal to the right hand side.\n$\\sin(x+y)=\\sin(x)\\cos(y)+\\cos(x)\\sin(y)\\leq \\sin(x)+\\sin(y) \\implies\n\\sin(x-\\pi)+\\sin(y)\\leq \\sin(x)-\\sin(y)\\implies \\sin(x-\\pi)\\geq -\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-y\\right)$\nNow, since $\\sin$ is an increasing function, this is true if and only if $(x-\\pi)\\leq 2\\pi-\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-y\\right)\\implies 2\\pi+2y-x\\leq \\pi\\implies y\\leq \\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}$\nTo ensure that this inequality is true for every $x$ between $0$ and $\\pi$, the right hand side should be within the bounds of the left, i.e.,\n$0\\leq \\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}\\leq \\pi\\implies 3\\pi\\leq 2x\\leq \\pi\\implies \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\nThus, $y\\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}]$ and $y\\in[\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi]$ (from rest of the problem) for every $x$ between $0$ and $\\pi$, inclusive.\nWe can write the solution as $y\\in[\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi]$, i.e., $y\\in[0,\\pi)$.\n\n<button disabled>Report Solution</button>",
